    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-70701-y",
    "abstract": "Mass extinctions in the early Palaeozoic have been attributed to global climate change and ocean anoxia with elevated phosphorus (P) proposed as a key driver. However, this hypothesis has lacked geochemical support due to the absence of proxies that can reconstruct changes in marine P availability. Focusing on the Late Ordovician Mass Extinction (LOME) and the Late Devonian Mass Extinction (LDME), we present carbonate-associated phosphate (CAP) data from seven globally distributed sections, providing a proxy record for seawater P variation across these events. Our data reveal short-lived, globally coherent P pulses that coincided with both events. These transient P surges align with biodiversity loss, widespread anoxia, and seawater temperature declines, suggesting a link between P flux, ocean anoxia, and global climate shifts, as supported by biogeochemical model results. These findings provide an empirical connection between brief marine P pulses and ecological crises during the LOME and LDME.",
    "field": "Biogeochemistry of mass extinctions",
    "problem": "The hypothesis that elevated marine phosphorus drove early Palaeozoic mass extinctions has lacked direct geochemical support due to the absence of proxies capable of reconstructing changes in ancient seawater phosphorus availability, leaving its role poorly constrained.",
    "method": "We applied carbonate-associated phosphate (CAP) analysis to seven globally distributed sections to reconstruct seawater phosphorus variations and provide empirical evidence for the role of phosphorus in driving Palaeozoic mass extinctions and climate change.",
    "task": "The task is to reconstruct marine phosphorus levels during the Late Ordovician and Late Devonian mass extinctions using carbonate-associated phosphate (CAP) data to investigate the hypothesized link between phosphorus surges, ocean anoxia, climate change, and biodiversity loss.",
    "model": [
        "SCION",
        "(py)SCION"
    ],
