            "paragraph": "Our CAP data support an increase in ocean P levels starting near the initial LOME and LDME events and peaking towards the end of the extinction event (Fig. 1). Our model results show that the increase in marine P availability (Fig. 3b and h) triggers a surge in marine primary productivity, which causes a positive model \u03b413CDIC (DIC: dissolved inorganic carbon) excursion of ~1-18\u2030, depending on the P input flux (Fig. 3c and i). Proxies from the geological record support this increase in productivity, such as elevated \u03b413Ccarb (Fig. 3c and i), biogenic barium, total organic carbon 48, zinc isotopes 49, and pyrite sulphur isotopes 50-52.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "nondimensional ocean-atmosphere component",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "atmospheric O2",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "The models show that the excess organic carbon burial, caused by increased P-limited productivity, increases atmospheric O2 levels (Fig. 3e and k), and decreases atmospheric CO2 levels, which in turn lowers global temperatures by up to around 5\u00b0C (Fig. 3f and I). This model result aligns with carbonate and apatite \u03b418O values in the LOME and LDME, that record 5\u20137\u00b0C seawater temperature drops 5,36.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
